  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 313 AM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nA low-level thermal ridge will briefly push northeastward \nacross the area today ahead of a cold front advancing from the \nUpper Midwest. This will bring warmer and slightly more humid \nconditions, with high temperatures reaching the mid to upper 80s\nand heat indices pushing into the upper 80s to near 90. The \nsurface cold front is projected to move southeastward through \nthe afternoon. However, dry antecedent air and a strong cap, \ncombined with weak large-scale support, should prevent \nconvective development. As a result, dry conditions will prevail\nthrough tonight.\n\nWinds will shift to the north tonight into Sunday behind the \nfront. The influx of a cooler airmass, increased cloud cover, \nand scattered afternoon showers driven by an approaching mid-\nlevel impulse will hold high temperatures 5 to 15 degrees \ncooler, generally in the 70s. Given the persistent dry air in \nthe lower levels, rainfall amounts will remain light, providing \nlittle beneficial rain.\n\nPrimarily dry conditions are expected Sunday night into Monday \nas the surface cold front settles across downstate IL and IN. \nThe best chance for rain should largely remain northwest of the \narea across the Upper Midwest as warm air advection increases \nahead of a potent shortwave trough and deepening surface low \ntracking from the northern High Plains into south-central \nCanada. The main impact from this system for us on Monday will \nbe increasingly breezy southeasterly winds through the day. \nOtherwise, Monday will be another seasonable day with \ntemperatures in the 70s under partly to mostly cloudy skies.\n\nThe cold front to our south is expected to lift back north \nacross the region Monday night as the strong low moves across \nfar south-central Canada. This will bring a brief return of \nwarm (80s) and humid conditions ahead of another approaching \ncold front on Tuesday. The airmass along this boundary will \nfeature precipitable water (PWAT) values of 1.7 to around 2.0 \ninches, supporting showers and storms capable of heavy rainfall.\nWe are closely monitoring forecast trends, as the front's \nsouthward progress may stall late Tuesday into Wednesday due to \nan expanding upper-level ridge over the southeastern CONUS. If \nit becomes quasi-stationary, it could focus multiple rounds of \nheavy rain producing strong storms across the lower Great Lakes\nregion through midweek\u2014a pattern resembling a classic Maddox \nfrontal heavy rain setup.\n\nConfidence in the position of the front remains low beyond \nWednesday due to large ensemble spread and potential disruption \nfrom earlier convection. However, additional waves of showers \nand thunderstorms will remain possible wherever the boundary \nsettles.\n\nKJB",
